{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791632722,"nonce":"mj4y3VFtSfOVTTE89htYVxoZxeQ=","title":"transform-origin reads two keywords by position, so `top center` scales from the left edge","message":"`parse_transform_origin` took the first token as x and the second as y whatever they were. `top center` came out as x = top = 0%, y = 50%: the middle of the left edge. CSS lets a keyword name its own axis, so `top center` is x 50%, y 0%. A single value was also copied to both axes, where CSS leaves the other axis at center (`25%` is `25% 50%`, `top` is `50% 0%`).\n\nSeven cn rules say `transform-origin: top center` (popover, hover card, dropdown and others), so their enter animations scaled from the left edge's middle instead of the top centre. `top left`, and the styling demo's `left top` / `right bottom`, came out right by luck.\n\nMeasured with the blinc_app GPU test `an_animated_panel_carries_its_text` (a 200 px panel scaling 0.5 -\u003e 1 about `top center`): the panel's horizontal centre is at 99.5 px at rest and 77.5 px mid-animation with the old parser; with the fix it stays put.\n\nTests: `blinc_css` `transform_origin_keywords_name_their_own_axis` (keyword pairs in both orders, single values, invalid pairs such as `left right`), and the centre assertion in the GPU test.","files":null}]}